<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Queen's Prayer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">22 March <a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/march-22">On this day: 22 March</a> · Also known as Ke Aloha O Ka Haku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a> (1838–1917), the last queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaiian Islands</a>, was one of Hawaiʻi's most accomplished composers and musicians; she wrote more than 165 songs and chants. &quot;The Queen's Prayer&quot;, in Hawaiian &quot;Ke Aloha O Ka Haku&quot;, is a mele she composed on 22 March 1895 while she was held under house arrest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a>, and dedicated to her niece and heir apparent, Victoria Kaʻiulani.</p></div>
